{"how-to-use":{"title":"How to use and cite *Bee-ing Human*","id":"how-to-use","parent":"about","link":"about/how-to-use","lead":"Read our quick, easy to use, manual on how to make the best of *Bee-ing Human*.","img":"assets/articles/how-to-use/bruce-warrington-Dqxfu4iehhU-unsplash.jpg","imgAlt":"close-up of a hatch door with printed instructions on how to use it","type":["article"],"layout":"article","author":["jenny"],"date":"2025/07/25","order":2},"image-credits":{"title":"Image Credits","id":"image-credits","parent":"about","link":"about/image-credits","lead":"A list of credits for all the images used in *Bee-ing Human*.","img":"assets/ben-marler-qQFuht8IFw0-unsplash.jpg","imgAlt":"a rock with writing on it that says, the cave","type":["data"],"layout":"article","author":"tiago","date":"2025/06/19","order":3},"introduction":{"title":"Introduction","id":"introduction","parent":"about","link":"about/introduction","lead":"Learn about the *Bee-ing Human* project, its goals, ambitions, ways of working and how we created a 21st century digital bee book.","img":"assets/articles/introduction/zulian-firmansyah-sEUAJeFn5mE-unsplash.jpg","imgAlt":"An open door to a dreamlike world on an orange wall","type":["article"],"layout":"article","author":["jenny","tiago","olivia"],"date":"2025/07/25","order":1},"people":{"title":"People","id":"people","parent":"about","link":"about/people","lead":"Meet the people behind *Bee-ing Human* and its extended network of collaborators.","img":"assets/jacqueline-o-gara-ZLkmJVCOYq8-unsplash.jpg","imgAlt":"a number of bees approaching the entrance to a wooden hive","type":["data"],"order":2},"project-timeline":{"title":"Project timeline","id":"project-timeline","parent":"about","link":"about/project-timeline","lead":"Here we detail the journey of the *Bee-ing Human* project, from the first discussions over a coffee, to the finished digital book you are reading today.","img":"assets/articles/project-timeline/sigmund-ZLst8z_M6_8-unsplash.jpg","imgAlt":"A series of calendar pages spread about","type":["article"],"layout":"article","author":["jenny","tiago"],"date":"2025/08/18","order":3},"beecoming-home":{"title":"Becoming Home: A blending of Art and Nature ","id":"beecoming-home","parent":"connections","link":"connections/beecoming-home","lead":"This project invited global artists to collaborate with bees by placing sculptures inside of active honeybee hives. The bees contribute by building honeycomb onto the artwork, creating living organic sculptures that highlight pollinator diversity and the beauty of interspecies collaboration.","img":"assets/articles/beecoming-home/image1.jpg","imgAlt":"honeycomb built on top of open books","type":["article"],"layout":"article","author":["luke","sarah"],"date":"2025/06/25"},"beekeepers-survey":{"title":"Beekeepers' Survey","id":"beekeepers-survey","parent":"connections","link":"connections/beekeepers-survey","lead":"Butler left his books in his study and chose instead to observe and listen to bees in the field. We are particularly interested in his study of swarms. We hope that today's beekeepers can add their knowledge to our project.","img":"assets/articles/beekeepers-survey/glenn-carstens-peters-RLw-UC03Gwc-unsplash.jpg","imgAlt":"a handwritten list with empty checkboxes","type":["article","experience","data"],"layout":"article","author":["bennett","jenny"],"date":"2025/04/11"},"connections-buzzwords":{"title":"Buzzwords feed","id":"connections-buzzwords","parent":"connections","link":"connections/buzzwords-feed","lead":"Buzzwords are the little thoughts and diversions we came across during our discussions and research. They might be tangential to our main topic, or an aside that seems pertinent enough to be recorded. Some of these buzzwords might turn into longer articles, others will remain here as a fleeting reflection.","img":"assets/glen-carrie-oHoBIbDj7lo-unsplash.jpg","imgAlt":"a view of a minaret from a ruined stone doorway or window","type":["experience"],"layout":false},"cool-tunes-podcast":{"title":"Cool Tunes For a Hot Planet Podcast","id":"cool-tunes-podcast","parent":"connections","link":"connections/cool-tunes-podcast","lead":"Produced by a team of like-minded musician/activists, the podcast explores the intersection of music and the struggle for social and environmental justice. The 'bees' episode grew from a belief that music from multiple places and times, but centered around universal human experiences, would create compelling and meaningful programming","img":"assets/articles/cool-tunes-podcast/will-francis-ZDNyhmgkZlQ-unsplash.jpg","imgAlt":"Description of the cover image","type":["article","media"],"layout":"article","author":"warren","date":"2025/05/19"},"connections-queen-bee":{"title":"Thought experiment: a queen bee in interdisciplinary space","id":"connections-queen-bee","parent":"connections","link":"connections/queen-bee","lead":"We've been looking at how this book thinks with bees, and how the writing uses all these modes to move around the bees creating emotional insight. Its polyphony is a prompt and a challenge. The many elements of the book naturally worked together in Butler's mind, but in 2024 they are distributed between specialisms in the departments of the university. Revisiting Butler's book with a team representing the different strands of his thinking offers a unique chance to see how what we now call 'interdisciplinarity' works","img":"assets/articles/queen-bee/leandro-fregoni-984b72nd5hg-unsplash.jpg","imgAlt":"black and yellow bee on white surface","type":["article"],"layout":"article","author":"olivia","date":"2025/01/20"},"vocalise-for-bees":{"title":"Vocalise (for bees)","id":"vocalise-for-bees","parent":"connections","link":"connections/vocalise-for-bees","lead":"Listen to Emily Doolittle's beautiful composition *vocalise (for bees)* performed by the Canadian soprano Rose Hegele.","img":"assets/articles/vocalise-for-bees/loc-dang-ZqznBx8W2g0-unsplash.jpg","imgAlt":"intentional movemont photo of an open mouth","type":["media"],"layout":"article","author":"emily","date":"2025/09/08"},"where-the-bee-sucks":{"title":"A film about bees and bee keeping ","id":"where-the-bee-sucks","parent":"connections","link":"connections/where-the-bee-sucks","lead":"*Where the Bee Sucks* is a 50 min film that looks at the year long cycle of bee keeping from the point of view of Ralph, the bee keeper.","img":"assets/articles/where-the-bee-sucks/screengrab.png","imgAlt":"A screen grab from Where the Bee Sucks, depicting the beekeeper, Ralph, tending to his hives dressed in a green smock and face veil.","type":["article","media"],"layout":"article","author":"adrin","date":"2025/04/24"},"bee-hold":{"title":"Bee-hold: Feeling the Buzz of Early Modern Natural Philosophy","id":"bee-hold","parent":"literature","link":"literature/bee-hold","lead":"The practice of 'feeling' is central to seventeenth-century scientific activity. Butler's chilled bee acts as a cautionary example of the ethical complexity of 'bee-holding', where 'feeling' as an interdisciplinary practice both permits and undermines mutual exchanges between the human and more-than-human.","img":"assets/articles/bee-hold/cover.jpeg","imgAlt":"A detail of a bee sting, from Robert Hooke's Micrographia","type":["article"],"layout":"article","author":"jess","date":"2025/07/08","order":3},"bees-early-modern-soundscape":{"title":"Bees in the Early Modern Soundscape: An Overview of Sound in Seventeenth-Century Beekeeping Books.","id":"bees-early-modern-soundscape","parent":"literature","link":"literature/bees-early-modern-soundscape","lead":"In this article, Bennett Hogg explores how the sound of bees was variously interpreted, used, and represented in apiculture manuals and other bee-writings of the seventeenth century.","img":"assets/articles/bees-early-modern-soundscape/michael-held-6n5XRyPBzAI-unsplash.jpg","imgAlt":"many bees filling the entire frame of the photo","type":["article"],"layout":"article","author":"bennett","date":"2025/09/05","order":4},"book-hive":{"title":"The Book as a Hive","id":"book-hive","parent":"literature","link":"literature/book-hive","lead":"The idea that we are entering a 'hive' when we open the 1623 edition is also suggested by this work’s material features, indicating Butler’s collaboration with the printer, Haviland. One of those features meets us as soon as we open the book: the new woodcut facing the titlepage.","img":"assets/articles/book-hive/hive-fp.jpg","imgAlt":"detail of the Feminine Monarchie frontespiece (1623) showing an illustration of a hive: a stylised series of hexagons with a few oversized bees over it. Around the hive the words: Solertia et Labore; below: Socordiam Lvimvs","type":["article"],"layout":"article","author":"jenny","date":"2025/01/03","order":2},"cross-references":{"title":"Cross-references","id":"cross-references","parent":"literature","link":"literature/cross-references","lead":"A draft visualisation of the internal cross-referencing systems in the 1623 and 1609 editions of the Feminine Monarchie","img":"assets/articles/cross-references/simon-kadula-DIwC450lRGI-unsplash.jpg","imgAlt":"a group of bees on top of a hive-like background","type":["data"],"author":"tiago","date":"2025/07/10","order":4},"shifting-relations-bees-humans":{"title":"'This So Profitable Government of Bees': Shifting Relations Between Bees and Humans in Early Modern Beekeeping Books","id":"shifting-relations-bees-humans","parent":"literature","link":"literature/shifting-relations-bees-humans","lead":"Although Butler's book was path breaking, it does not appear out of nowhere. In this essay, Bennett samples a century of English beekeeping books, from Hyll through to Rusden, to understand some of the changes in beekeeping practices and attitudes that took place during the seventeenth century.","img":"assets/articles/shifting-relations-bees-humans/paolo-tognoni-h7PO6YUD9uI-unsplash.jpg","imgAlt":"Gloved hands holding a bee hive","type":["article"],"layout":"article","author":"bennett","date":"2025/11/04","order":4},"transcription":{"title":"*The Feminine Monarchie*","id":"transcription","parent":"literature","link":"literature/transcription","lead":"The scholarly digital edition of Charles Butler's *The Feminine Monarchie* (1623), by Jennifer Richards, Olivia Smith, and Tiago Sousa Garcia","img":"assets/andrew-seaman--m88z7ily-w-unsplash.jpg","imgAlt":"a picture of a typewriter","dataSelector":true,"dataSelectorControls":[{"dataSource":true,"type":"select","label":"dataSource","default":"1623","values":{"1609":"1609","1623":"1623","1634":"1634"}},{"dataSource":false,"type":"navigator","listIndex":[["Titlepage","titlepage"],["Preface","preface"],["Ad Autorem","dedication"],["Table of Contents","contents"],["Chapter 1","ch1"],["Chapter 2","ch2"],["Chapter 3","ch3"],["Chapter 4","ch4"],["Chapter 5","ch5"],["Chapter 6","ch6"],["Chapter 7","ch7"],["Chapter 8","ch8"],["Chapter 9","ch9"],["Chapter 10","ch10"]],"defaultSelected":"titlepage"},{"dataSource":false,"type":"radioGroup","label":"view","defaultValue":"both","values":{"facsimile":"facsimile","both":"both","transcription":"transcription"}},{"dataSource":false,"type":"radioGroup","label":"variation","defaultValue":"no variation","values":{"no variation":"no variation","major changes":"major changes","all changes":"all changes"}},{"dataSource":false,"type":"toggle","label":"editorial notes","values":{"default":false}},{"dataSource":false,"type":"toggle","label":"translations","values":{"default":false}}],"type":["data","experience"],"order":1},"vocal-cues":{"title":"Vocal Cues: Charles Butler's Phonetic Orthography","id":"vocal-cues","parent":"literature","link":"literature/vocal-cues","lead":"Rachel Willie discusses Charles Butler's proposed new English orthography in which, much like in *Melissomelos*, Butler attempts to capture and represent the sounds of the language as they are heard.","img":"assets/articles/vocal-cues/cover.jpg","imgAlt":"a few pieces of metal type with the letter n","type":["article"],"layout":"article","author":"rachel","date":"2025/09/08","order":4},"EPV-performance":{"title":"Ensemble Pro Victoria Performance","id":"EPV-performance","parent":"music","link":"music/EPV-performance","lead":"Ensemble Pro Victoria bring Charles Butler’s Melissomelos to life in Newcastle University’s King’s Hall, February 2024.","img":"assets/articles/EPV-performance/EPV-at-New-Coll.png","imgAlt":"choir singing in a darkened church","type":["media"],"layout":"article","author":"magnus","date":"2025/04/03"},"butler-composer-oxford":{"title":"Charles Butler the Composer, 1: his Oxford years","id":"butler-composer-oxford","parent":"music","link":"music/butler-composer-oxford","lead":"In this study, Magnus investigates Butler's years in Oxford: the years that shaped his thinking and working methods but are easily misconstrued, in particular, the timing of Butler's career at Magdalen College.","img":"assets/articles/butler-composer-oxford/may_morning.jpg","imgAlt":"A painting of choristers in front of Magdalen tower","type":["article"],"layout":"article","author":"magnus","date":"2025/08/19"},"butler-composer-rationale":{"title":"Charles Butler the Composer, 2: the Rationale of *Melissomelos*","id":"butler-composer-rationale","parent":"music","link":"music/butler-composer-rationale","lead":"In this study, Magnus investigates Charles Butler's only two surviving compositions, and discusses the peculiarities of *Melissomelos*, including some of its typographical idiosyncrasies.","img":"assets/articles/butler-composer-rationale/ASH_ASHM_WA1936_222-001.jpg","imgAlt":"A painting of an Interior with a young Man holding a Recorder, by Cecco del Caravaggio (b.c.1589)","type":["article"],"layout":"article","author":"magnus","date":"2025/08/29"},"chapel-in-the-hive":{"title":"Chapel in the Hive","id":"chapel-in-the-hive","parent":"music","link":"music/chapel-in-the-hive","lead":"Joseph Campana writes about his collaboration with Kurt Stallmann in 'The Chapel in the Hive', a performance of poetry inspired by an intriguing passage from Charles Butler's *Feminine Monarchie*.","img":"assets/articles/chapel-in-the-hive/DSC_0609-67.jpg","imgAlt":"A picture of the interior ceiling of La Sagrada Familia in Barcelona","type":["article","media"],"layout":"article","author":"joseph","date":"2025/08/04"},"charles-butlers-feminine-monarchie":{"title":"Charles Butler's *Feminine Monarchie*","id":"charles-butlers-feminine-monarchie","parent":"music","link":"music/charles-butlers-feminine-monarchie","lead":"Jennifer Richards (Cambridge) and Magnus Williamson (Newcastle) discuss 'The Feminine Monarchie, or The History of Bees' (London, 1623), and its extraordinary bee song, 'Melissomelos', with Simon Jackson (Peterhouse, Cambridge) and Richard Wistreich (Royal College of Music). ","img":"assets/articles/charles-butlers-feminine-monarchie/20250523_142642.jpg","imgAlt":"A shot of the preview screen of a recording camera","type":["media"],"layout":"article","author":["jenny","magnus","richard","simon-jackson"],"date":"2025/08/28"},"melisosomelos-edition":{"title":"*Melissomelos*","id":"melisosomelos-edition","parent":"music","link":"music/melissomelos-edition","lead":"Complete edition by Magnus Williamson for the *Bee-ing Human project* (Leverhulme Trust, 2022-25). First performed complete by Ensemble Pro Victoria, Newcastle University, 29 February 2024.","img":"assets/articles/melissomelos-edition/2023-1081_0093.jpg","imgAlt":"A music page from Butlers Feminine Monarchie","type":["data"],"layout":"article","author":"magnus","date":"2025/06/06"},"music-book-layout":{"title":"Music-book Layout: Functionality and Sociability","id":"music-book-layout","parent":"music","link":"music/music-book-layout","lead":"The musicians need to be positioned so they can hear – and ideally, see – one another extremely well. This means close physical proximity, which in turn enhances the particular social intimacy of making music from individual parts. The few early modern images we have of part-book or choir-book performances show just this: the musicians are usually standing close together or sitting clustered around a table or music stand and are often touching one another.","img":"assets/articles/music-book-layout/louie_detail.jpeg","imgAlt":"needs an alt","type":["article"],"layout":"article","author":"richard","date":"2025/06/09"},"performing-melissomelos":{"title":"Performing *Melissomelos*","id":"performing-melissomelos","parent":"music","link":"music/performing-melissomelos","lead":"While revisiting his first encounter with *Melissomelos*, Simon Jackson explores his approach in performing Butler's song of Bees.","img":"assets/articles/performing-melissomelos/susan-wilkinson-fMXyMuOxuYU-unsplash.jpg","imgAlt":"An abstract painting of soundwaves in blue and cream tones","type":["article","media"],"layout":"article","author":"simon-jackson","date":"2025/08/05"},"sound-in-feminine-monarchie":{"title":"Sound in *The Feminine Monarchie* (1623)","id":"sound-in-feminine-monarchie","parent":"music","link":"music/sound-in-feminine-monarchie","lead":"Bennett discusses how Butler's training in the arts of grammar, rhetoric, and music predisposes him to attend to sound and how, in *The Feminine Monarchie*, sound plays a number of roles that, for Butler, afford insights into the world of the bees.","img":"assets/articles/sound-in-feminine-monarchie/md-mahdi-VlE1L22HS_I-unsplash.jpg","imgAlt":"An abstract light painting of soundwaves in black and gold","type":["article"],"layout":"article","author":"bennett","date":"2025/09/10"},"conscious-beeing":{"title":"The honey bee: a conscious bee-ing?","id":"conscious-beeing","parent":"science","link":"science/conscious-beeing","lead":"Bees are a flagship species in the study of invertebrate consciousness. Beloved by the public for their ecological importance and complex social behaviors, they provide a unique opportunity to engage society in the broader discussion about animal consciousness.","img":"assets/articles/conscious-beeing/alexander-grey-p203ekCK4Ac-unsplash.jpg","imgAlt":"Psychedelic colorful rainbow paint wallpaper background.","type":["article"],"layout":"article","author":["catherine","luigi","martin"],"date":"2025/04/10"},"science-datasets":{"title":"Datasets","id":"science-datasets","parent":"science","link":"science/datasets","lead":"Here you will find the raw data for some of the experiments we ran in the bee lab, for you to explore in various forms, validate our interpretations, and draw your own conclusions.","img":"assets/pexels-murillohm-10854279.jpg","imgAlt":"a neatly arranged pile of wood","type":["data"],"dataSelector":true,"dataSelectorControls":[{"dataSource":true,"type":"select","label":"dataSource","default":0,"values":{"Dataset 1":0}},{"dataSource":false,"type":"radioGroup","label":"view","defaultValue":"details","values":{"details":"details","visualisation":"visualisation","summary":"summary","data":"data"}}],"layout":false},"emotions-in-invertebrates":{"title":"Inner life – emotions – in invertebrates","id":"emotions-in-invertebrates","parent":"science","link":"science/emotions-in-invertebrates","lead":"The study of emotions in invertebrates has been a subject of both intrigue and scepticism. While challenging our anthropocentric notions, the investigation of emotions in invertebrates promises to broaden the understanding of the evolutionary origins and adaptive functions of these phenomena across the animal kingdom.","img":"assets/articles/emotions-in-invertebrates/bioscience-image-library-by-fayette-reynolds-wDH7c3pXkpY-unsplash.jpg","imgAlt":"Nervous Tissue: Spinal Cord Motor Neuron. smear: spinal cord magnification: 200x","type":["article"],"layout":"article","author":["luigi","vivek"],"date":"2025/03/03"},"studying-bumblebee-cognition":{"title":"Studying Bumblebee Cognition","id":"studying-bumblebee-cognition","parent":"science","link":"science/studying-bumblebee-cognition","lead":"Unlike Charles Butler, our research focused on bumblebees rather than honeybees. Vivek explains the advantages of working with bumblebees and the history of research on honeybees and bumblebees.","img":"assets/articles/studying-bumblebee-cognition/cover.jpeg","imgAlt":"A bumblebee on a flower","type":["article"],"layout":"article","author":"vivek","date":"2025/11/10"}}